  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM CDT Sun Sep 6 2026\n\nTODAY THROUGH TUESDAY...Ridging remains in place across the\ncentral plains this morning, with a corridor of greater \nsurface moisture stretching from the Gulf Coast up through the \nDakotas. The Marginal Risk from the Day 1 SWO does look a little\ngoofy, though it does match up with this region of greater \nmoisture and instability. Zooming into our area, the greatest \nchance for storm development today will be across western MN. \nTiming and coverage are still pretty low confidence though. The\nremnants/energy from the storms ongoing across western Nebraska\nthis morning are the most likely trigger for storms this \nafternoon in Minnesota and Iowa. You see this idea with the \nlatest runs of the HRRR and RRFS, where storms bubble along the \naxis of higher instability. There are reasonable scenarios where\nstorms are relatively limited in coverage, but have a greater \nchance of producing marginally severe wind or hail... or you get\nwidely scattered storms that are mostly garden variety with a \nfew becoming more organized at points. Regardless of how \nwidespread in coverage the storms become, the drop-off in \nMUCAPE values to the east will struggle to support any storms \nfrom advancing much farther than the line from St Cloud to \nMankato.\n\nAfter storms dissipate some time Sunday evening, it will be a \nrelatively quiet end to the long weekend until our final wave \nof this pattern moves through overnight Monday into Tuesday. \n\nA potent shortwave trough will break us out of this hot and\nstormy pattern, bringing one last push for rain. The trough \nitself will not be orientated as favorably for a high end \nsevere threat, though the SPC does have a Marginal Risk for Days\n2-3 covering the Monday night to Tuesday morning window. The \ngreatest rainfall amounts are still favored along the \ninternational border closer to the surface low, though we will \ncertainly take any moisture we can squeeze out from the cold \nfront that sweeps across the southern half of the state and into\nwestern WI. The majority of the morning actively should be \nmostly wrapped up by noon, leaving us in a cooler and drier \nairmass heading into the rest of the week.\n\nWEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY...Seasonable temperatures and mostly\ndry conditions look to be the main story for the second half \nof the week. Northwest flow aloft may support a weak wave from \nsparking small rain chances any day, though there is no real \nstrong signal from any long range ensemble guidance. As we get \nto the end of the weekend and into next week, there are some \nsigns pointing towards a negative PNA phase which could support \nan even bigger push into fall-like weather for the central US. \nHeck, there are trees near the office already starting to drop \ntheir leaves, though it is more out of moisture preservation at\nthis point...",
